  </ol></p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="act-4-3-5"><title>Atmospheric Pressure</title>

<introduction><p>The atmospheric pressure decreases with altitude above the surface of the earth, according to the formula 
        <md>P(h) = 30(10^{-0.09h})</md>   
  where <m>h</m> is altitude in miles and <m>P</m> is atmospheric pressure in inches of mercury.</p></introduction>

  <p><ol marker="a.">
      <li><p>Use technology to graph this function for  <m>0 \le h \le 10</m>.</p>

        <aside component="instructor"><p>Emphasize the difference between part (b), where we evaluate the exponential function, and part (c), where we solve an exponential equation. Point out that many application problems are really asking for one or the other of these operations.</p></aside></li>

    <li><p>Use algebra to answer the question, then verify on your graph:</p>
        <p>The elevation of Mount McKinley, the highest mountain in the United Sates, is 20,320  feet. What is the atmospheric pressure at the top? (Hint:  1 mile = 5280 feet.)</p></li>

    <li><p>Use algebra to answer the question, then verify on your graph:</p>
        <p>How high above sea level is the atmospheric pressure 16.1 inches of mercury?</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>
 
<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
<li><p>What is the only difference in the steps for solving the two equations: 
    <me>5x^{10} - 8 = 496~~~~~~\text{and}~~~~~~5(10^x)-8 = 496</me></p></li>      
<li><p>Compute <m>\dfrac{\log{8}}{\log{3}}</m> and <m>\log{\dfrac\left{8}{3}}\right</m>. Are they the same?</p></li>
<li><p>What happens when you apply an operation to a number, then apply the inverse operation?</p></li>
<li><p>Explain how to use the graph of <m>y=3^x</m>  to estimate the value of <m>\log_3 {12}</m>.</p></li>
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Explain how to estimate <m>\log_{10}{x}</m> between two integers.</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain how to use a graph of <m>y=3^x</m> to estimate <m>\log_3{12}</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain why <m>\log_b{1} = 0</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>If <m>\log_2{x}</m> is negative, what can you say about <m>x</m>?</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>A logarithm is the same as</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>an exponent.</li> 
        <li>a coefficient.</li>
        <li>a quotient.</li>  
        <li>a radical.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\log_b{b} = 1</m> because</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>\dfrac{b}{b} = 1</m></li>
        <li><m>\sqrt[b]{b}=1</m></li>
        <li><m>b^1=b</m></li>
        <li><m>1^b=1</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>Which statement is true?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>The log of a number is never negative.</li>
        <li>We cannot take the log of a negative number.</li>
        <li>The log of a fraction is called a common log.</li>
        <li>The log of 0 is 1.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>What is the first step in solving the equation <m>~5(10)^x = 12</m>?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>Multiply 5 times 10.</li>
        <li>Take the log of both sides.</li>
        <li>Get zero on one side of the equation.</li>
        <li>Divide both sides by 5.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>